    "turn_idx": 8,
    "turn_text": "Well, there's a lot of choices there. I share your concern because I remember, you know, a good portion of the 20th century. I remember parts of the Depression, the tail end of the Depression, World War II, the Korean War, the Vietnam War. I was in the military. And then I came up to having all those wars in the Middle East. So I've seen a lot of that and been concerned about it all. And a lot of people shared those concerns. I mean, I was drafted during the Cold War. And I thought that was a serious problem. I was in a residency and they took me out of my residency and there were missiles in Cuba. And I didn't know how scared you should be, but it was dangerous. But now it's different. I don't think we have faced that same danger. But I think the problems are every bit serious. And I see it differently because I see it more as a cultural and moral crisis that we have. I have now. I've always been skeptical of government in all ways and the CIA and FBI. But right now I think More and more people are starting to realize how powerful they are and going through and I've been sort of fascinated with reading about the Kennedy assassination and that makes me realize how How inept the people themselves are because the the secret government is is a big deal and I've been following that for a long time the secret government of the of the Federal Reserve system and money and try to understand that in a Historic sense and I'll tell you those problems are worse So, you know the loss of our liberties probably it's at the greatest threat even with all those wars going on and we always lost a Liberty's during wartime. Sometimes we cut recovered a little bit, but I think right now it's it's it's really bad. And it's very dangerous because I think we're morally bankrupt. And I think that leads to a financial bankruptcy and then a distortion of the economy. And it leads to these unbelievable deficits. And we've been trained. Our country's been taught by economists and people. Our government run the universities teaching this stuff. And it's a religion. The deficits don't matter. And the only thing that matters is political power. So under those circumstances, I think I feel less secure and I think our liberties are endangered. Yet at the same time, even just a few minutes ago during my report, I usually try to end up by saying, people are waking up and you can wake up. It's conceivable, but you better prepare for the worst. But I'll tell you what, I work on the assumption that people who can and will and should but I don't do it with thinking, boy, if I just could get back into Congress, maybe they'll listen to me. I never had that idea before I went to Congress or while I was there, it's just that that isn't where the action is. The action is reaching people, doing things like you're doing to reach people and try to explain things because I think it's an educational effort. That's why I'm a strong supporter of the Mises Institute and any institute that's going to teach free market economics at the same time, the protection of liberty.",
