  {
    "episode_id": 48899,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48899.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, the people are going to wake up and want to reclaim their own liberties. You and I, what we have to do is reclaim our liberty the best we can. So that is a major, major job. and the chances of us doing it by ourselves, but people have to have leadership and they have to have principle. The one thing about the time of our revolution and most revolutions are led by a few people, both bad and good. They only need five or six percent of the thought leaders and this is this, this is it. And then they rally the troops and and get them to support the ideas, they lie and cheat in doing this. But governments don't exist without the support of the people. So we have to have people, and I think that's why I like to read a lot about the mentality of the intellectualism of our founders of this country. They were pretty smart people. They wrote a good document, but there were some shortcomings from the beginning with the Constitution, but they were brilliant compared to everybody else. They did a great job, but it held up for a good bit, but it's really gone down mainly because the people haven't had concern But I like the idea that it's an intellectual battle. We have to deal in principles. We have to have leaders expressing these views. And then we have to galvanize people. And this is why I have this grain of optimism, what's going on. Like even today, two or three examples on my program. where people are saying enough is enough and one place like it was in Italy where the police were out there to stop the people who were resisting and disobeying the government and they took their helmets and their guns off and later joined the people who were descending. And you just have to encourage people not to submit. I love it when people go to a school board meeting, sometimes by themselves, sometimes with a group, and they stand up, you know, to the bullies. You know, these political bullies that write these laws and people are sick and tired of them. And all of a sudden, you know, there was just one recently where the where the school board threw up their hands and yelled and screamed and they got upset and they finally had to stop the meeting and leave. So it's getting people to stand up for what they should be because there's a lot more people who believe it. All they need is a little bit of encouragement but we still have to have leaders that will stand up and say this is what we have to do and Hopefully we can keep this momentum going in the direction of saying this has been crazy stuff, especially this last year or two, you know, with no respect at all for civil liberties, no respect at all for any sensible economic policy, no respect. Of course, this is going on a long time for other nations. The empire has to end. And those are the kind of things that I found college students were very open to. and they used to accuse me of being anti-military, because I didn't want the wars, I didn't want to finance them. It turns out that in my campaign, I got more money from the military than all the other candidates put together. And I didn't even know that would be the case. So I think you have to understand what you believe in and make sure that it's defending For me, defending the principles of liberty and when you can use the Constitution for that purpose and economic policy, use it. But you've got to get people excited right now. I'm trying to get people excited that don't submit. Don't submit. Stand up to them and do it in a peaceful manner and others will join. So the numbers are not too devastating about people who are sick and tired of all this.",
