  {
    "episode_id": 48899,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48899.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 21,
    "turn_text": "I agree. And another thing that's really bothered me in the recent year and a half has been watching medical doctors, PhDs, et cetera, make the intellectual cases, you know, Maybe that not everybody needs a vaccine or what have you or maybe that some people shouldn't take it and having the silence by, you know, like basically interns at social media. I, when I watch your son on the, on the floor of the Senate, be told he doesn't understand science and medicine. And I knew no full well, you yourself are a medical doctor. He's a medical doctor. This just doesn't jive for me. Like to me, the, the entire point of science, because science has now been presented to us as a authority. versus what's like science is a process. It's not an authority actually read a paper out MIT that said it was a problem that we us normal free thing and people think science is a process. We don't see it as an authority. I'm like, I don't even know what to say to that. And if you look at this scientifically, we should be having vigorous academic debate. I've heard a lot of things about hydroxychloroquine. I've heard a lot of things about Ivermectin. What I haven't seen is two professionals in a professional environment debate the issue at a professional level. I've seen talking head streaking on media, but I haven't seen, you know, two people well-informed. And I think without that rigorous debate, we don't have science and we don't have true academia. We have basically like a cult of modern personality.",
