  {
    "episode_id": 48899,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48899.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "You know, I think you put that very well because in medical school, as well as my residency, there was a lot of debates. But in fact, debates are what we look forward to because we didn't have a couple professors and they had some disagreement on how you treat people, you know, the details of it. And for them to get up in an academic setting, we really enjoy hearing those debates. And even as a resident, I can remember there were two drugs that had come out, had to do with stopping labor and the pros and cons of that. So they would assign one position on one doctor and something on another. I remember participating in those things. And that's what science should be about. And you make that point. We should be talking about that. The one thing for sure. is having a bureaucrat who's never seen a patient in his life dictating all the rules and regulations what all the other doctors can do and punish you if you don't do it and you can lose your job over it. It is really bad. And I think the doctors are starting to speak out. It's this meaning that they just held in Rome. There's over 4,000 people now that are just doctors who are saying enough is enough. We're not going to put up with this. They needed to be reminded of the Hippocratic oath. and how that works. But the one word for me is the issue of volunteerism to solve. How do you solve this problem? Somebody has this desire, somebody has this desire. Well, we'll decide on which church we're going to go to. Yeah,",
