    "turn_text": "But then when this campaign started, the presidential campaign, the thing that I was most pleased with, I have to admit it, was the crowd I got at Berkeley. I think we had 8,000 people there. It's not like a Donald Trump rally, but it was really significant because of the nature of and a reputation of Berkeley, but I gave them this exactly the same message as I would to talk to Democrats, liberals, libertarians or Republicans. It was always the same thing. There's always something in there for everybody, and the main thing is that I am so convinced that a free society has so much to offer that it's a shame that we don't do a better job in selling it to the people and encouraging people And that's why I get a lot of compliments from young people. I say, well, you're the ones that encourage me because you're excited about it. So that was the way it worked for me. And I think that everybody has a responsibility if they have any beliefs or convictions to get as well as just as you can about how the market works. Leonard Reed taught me he was a head of foundation for economic education. He says, get knowledgeable on something. He said, you won't have to worry ever about finding people to talk to. He says, eventually they'll come to hear you. And it was more of a less just an encouragement because if you go by how many people, so everybody can find one person or 10 people or 20 people, you've started a program what 15 or 20 years ago, and you've been doing it ever since and you're talking about these issues. So that's what's good. Everybody has that responsibility. So often after rally, People would come up young people kind of said okay. Yeah, tell me what I should do I agree with you And I said do whatever you want to do is whatever you're comfortable with write a book run for office Help somebody else in office or whatever you want to do. I just do it and there's there's a place for everybody Sometimes you don't think you're doing much and sometimes you're doing a lot more than you realize",
