    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48899.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "You know, book burning has been around for a long time because if people see a challenge, especially the authoritarian are the ones who burn books because they want their power and their authority to run other people's lives, So the book burning doesn't work only temporarily. It's an evil thing to do. And I just don't think that they can. I'm not very knowledgeable on technology, on internet, but I really believe in it. And it's a mess. I mean, social media is a big problem. But I think it's big. And I use it. And we have some problems. But I think I give a lot of credit to the people who know a lot more about that technology because my staff can tell me, well, you know, you can do this. You could push stuff over here, over here. And I don't, I have, I like it when people say they can't close down the Internet, you know. So they can put obstacles in there and there will be problems. But basically it's a tool. They could burn books but they could find another piece of paper and write another book. So I think there's going to be technology that's going to be able to allow us to keep going and present our case because I never dreamed that I could reach so many people. Just in my own little office here and like Jackson, you know and one day not too long ago I had an interview not not on my channel I won't try to say my channel is that big but you know I did an interview with somebody and they kind of over a million people watched it. Where did they come from? And that wasn't even, I don't know whether, I don't know whether that was in, that probably was on YouTube. Who knows how they got distributed, but there are a lot of different ways that can be distributed. So I think that's there. And I think that the most important thing is there, do you have a message that's powerful? I think, you know, going back to my wife's comments, I said, she said, no, you're going to win. And I said, well, why is that? She says, because my argument was you can't kill Santa Claus again. She says, they're going to know you're telling the truth. A lot of people have mentioned that to me. A lot of people, supporters in my district, so often they would come up because I was challenging the status quo. And they would come up and say, Ron, I like what you're doing. I disagree with you on this, and I disagree with you that. But you know what? You always tell us the truth. We know where you stand. So I think truth it, because you can't get two people, most times spouses don't agree on every issue, you know. So there's a lot of disagreements. It's a spallagerism that you have to have to emphasize. But what the people want is somebody to tell them the truth. And what is really, really absent today in our whole system is nobody's believable. Almost nobody. There's a few up there. are believable, but no truth is not very readily available. So if nothing else, people need to grasp some basic principles and just go out and talk to people or write to people, whatever you're capable of doing, and give them a truthful message. And don't worry. The last thing I ever worry about is never motivated for a second to get to go to Congress day a long time and become a committee chairman. Go to the banking committee and get rid of the Fed. Never, never that. But I ended up staying a lot",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
